---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<ProjectLayout
  title="Cadaster AI Assistant"
  year="2026"
  section="Data Science & Analysis"
  category="Personal Project · Proof of Concept"
  tagline="A proof of concept for a controlled conversational interface to cadastral data — ask a normal-language question, get an answer grounded in curated data, without ever handing the AI unrestricted database access."
  liveUrl="https://cadaster-ai-frontend.netlify.app/"
  liveLabel="Open live demo"
  githubUrl="https://github.com/ofer-geo/cadaster-ai-assistant"
  tools={['Python', 'FastAPI', 'LangChain', 'LangGraph', 'dbt', 'PostGIS', 'Google Gemini', 'React', 'MapLibre GL']}
>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">The Problem</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        Working with clients on cadastral and land-information systems, I kept seeing the same
        pattern: users can only inspect cadastral data through predefined forms, filters, tables,
        reports or GIS layers. These workflows are structured and necessary — but they assume the
        user already knows which table holds the answer, which layer to open, which filter to
        apply, which spatial operation to run, and how the underlying data model is put together.
      </p>
      <p>
        A user often knows the business question they want to ask — "what parcels overlap?",
        "what happened to this parcel?" — without knowing how to translate it into the system's
        menus and filters. That gap between the question someone has and the query the system
        expects is what this POC set out to explore.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">The Product Idea</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        What if a user could ask a cadastral database a normal-language question, while the system
        kept strict control over what the AI is actually allowed to access and execute?
      </p>
      <p>
        The Cadaster AI Assistant uses an LLM purely as an <strong>interpretation layer</strong>
        between the user and the cadastral data — not as a database client. The model does not
        receive unrestricted SQL access and has no freedom to query the database directly. It reads
        the user's intent and decides which of a small set of predefined, trusted tools to call;
        everything the tools are allowed to do is fixed in advance. This gets natural-language
        interaction without giving the AI unrestricted access to the underlying cadastral database.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="proj-flow" role="img" aria-label="Flow: User asks a question, the LLM agent interprets it and selects a tool, the tool queries a controlled dbt model in PostGIS, and the agent returns an answer plus a map visualization">
      <span class="proj-flow-step">User question</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">LLM agent<br /><small>interprets intent</small></span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Approved tool<br /><small>deterministic call</small></span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">dbt model / view<br /><small>curated, controlled</small></span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">PostGIS</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-arrow">→</span>
      <span class="proj-flow-step">Answer + map</span>
    </div>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        The agent's job stops at understanding the question and picking the right tool. It never
        writes or executes SQL itself. The tool queries a curated dbt model, the structured result
        comes back, and the agent explains that result in natural language — with any spatial
        results passed to the frontend as GeoJSON and drawn on the map. Database access stays
        deterministic and controlled at every step.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">POC Capabilities</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        This is a proof of concept, not a production cadastral system. The current build implements
        a small set of tools demonstrating several kinds of cadastral queries:
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="proj-list">
      <li>Retrieving current parcels</li>
      <li>Filtering parcels by district and status</li>
      <li>Viewing parcel history and lineage (splits, retirements, version chains)</li>
      <li>Identifying parcel overlaps</li>
      <li>Retrieving parcel statistics</li>
      <li>Returning spatial results that can be visualized on a map</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>Things you can ask the assistant in the live demo:</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="proj-quotes">
      <li>"Can you show me the current approved parcels in District Mjini?"</li>
      <li>"What overlaps exist in the cadastral database?"</li>
      <li>"Can you show me the history of parcel PAR-101?"</li>
    </ul>

    <img
      src={`${base}/maps/cadaster-ai-assistant-3.png`}
      alt="Chat interface asking the assistant to summarize cadaster database statistics, returning total, current, approved, in-process and retired parcel counts, alongside a map overview of the Zanzibar coastline"
      class="proj-map"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <p class="proj-map-caption">
      Database statistics on request — the tool returns structured counts, the agent narrates them.
    </p>

    <img
      src={`${base}/maps/cadaster-ai-assistant-1.png`}
      alt="Chat interface asking what overlaps exist in the cadastral database, returning a table of overlapping parcel pairs with their statuses, alongside a map highlighting the overlapping parcel geometries"
      class="proj-map"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <p class="proj-map-caption">
      Overlap detection — the agent selects the overlap tool, and the result is rendered on the map.
    </p>

    <img
      src={`${base}/maps/cadaster-ai-assistant-2.png`}
      alt="Chat interface asking for the history of parcel PAR-100, returning its version status and validity dates plus a lineage summary of the child parcels it was split into, alongside a street map showing the parcel's location"
      class="proj-map"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <p class="proj-map-caption">
      Parcel history and lineage — split events, retirements and successor parcels explained in plain language.
    </p>

    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        The frontend is intentionally simple. Its purpose is to demonstrate the interaction pattern
        — conversational result to spatial visualization — rather than to be a full cadastral client.
        The same pattern could later be embedded into an existing cadastral information system, land
        information system, GIS portal or administrative application.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Architecture &amp; Engineering Decisions</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>The architecture is deliberately designed around one constraint: the LLM interprets intent, and only trusted, predefined operations ever touch the database.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="proj-list">
      <li><strong>No free-form SQL.</strong> The LLM cannot freely query PostgreSQL and never generates arbitrary SQL for execution — it can only call predefined tools.</li>
      <li><strong>Curated data layer.</strong> Tools query curated views and models built with dbt, so the data layer is controlled separately from the AI layer.</li>
      <li><strong>Grounded answers.</strong> Tool outputs — not model recall — provide the grounding for every final answer, reducing hallucination risk.</li>
      <li><strong>Spatial results as GeoJSON.</strong> Results that carry geometry are passed back to the frontend as GeoJSON and rendered on a MapLibre map.</li>
      <li><strong>Tracing.</strong> Agent runs are traced end-to-end with LangSmith, so tool selection and reasoning steps are inspectable rather than opaque.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Technology Stack</h2>

    <div class="proj-stack-grid">
      <div class="proj-stack-group">
        <h3 class="proj-stack-label">Database</h3>
        <ul class="proj-stack-list">
          <li>PostgreSQL</li>
          <li>PostGIS</li>
          <li>Aiven managed PostgreSQL/PostGIS (deployed POC)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="proj-stack-group">
        <h3 class="proj-stack-label">Data Engineering</h3>
        <ul class="proj-stack-list">
          <li>dbt</li>
          <li>Raw cadastral source tables</li>
          <li>Staging models</li>
          <li>Curated marts / views for the application</li>
          <li>ELT workflow separating raw data from the application-facing layer</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="proj-stack-group">
        <h3 class="proj-stack-label">Backend / AI</h3>
        <ul class="proj-stack-list">
          <li>Python &amp; FastAPI</li>
          <li>LangChain &amp; LangGraph</li>
          <li>Google Gemini</li>
          <li>LangSmith (tracing &amp; observability)</li>
          <li>Psycopg for PostgreSQL access</li>
          <li>Purpose-built cadastral query tools</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="proj-stack-group">
        <h3 class="proj-stack-label">Frontend</h3>
        <ul class="proj-stack-list">
          <li>React &amp; Vite</li>
          <li>MapLibre GL</li>
          <li>React Markdown</li>
          <li>GeoJSON-based map updates</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="proj-stack-group">
        <h3 class="proj-stack-label">Deployment</h3>
        <ul class="proj-stack-list">
          <li>Netlify — frontend</li>
          <li>Render — FastAPI backend</li>
          <li>Aiven — PostgreSQL/PostGIS</li>
          <li>GitHub — source control &amp; deployment integration</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <div class="proj-note">
      <h2 class="proj-note-title">A note on the data</h2>
      <p>
        All data used in this project is <strong>synthetic</strong>. The demo dataset is loosely
        based on a Zanzibar cadastral context because I was physically working in Zanzibar while
        developing this POC — but no parcels, ownership records, boundaries, IDs, names or other
        information shown in the application represent real cadastral records. The dataset exists
        only for demonstration and portfolio purposes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">What This Could Become</h2>
    <div class="proj-body">
      <p>
        This project is intentionally limited to a proof of concept. But the interaction model it
        demonstrates — natural-language questions answered through controlled, predefined tools
        over curated data — isn't specific to this dataset. The same pattern could be extended with
        a broader tool set and embedded into a real cadastral or land-information system, giving
        non-technical users a way to explore data conversationally without ever loosening control
        over what the AI is allowed to touch.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="proj-section">
    <h2 class="proj-section-title">Code</h2>
    <p class="proj-body">
      Source is public on GitHub; the demo is deployed live.
    </p>
    <div class="proj-links">
      <a class="proj-external proj-external-primary" href="https://cadaster-ai-frontend.netlify.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Open live demo →
      </a>
      <a class="proj-external" href="https://github.com/ofer-geo/cadaster-ai-assistant" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        View repository on GitHub →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

</ProjectLayout>

<style>
  :global(.proj-list) {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }
  :global(.proj-list li) {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #2A2A2A;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
    position: relative;
  }
  :global(.proj-list li::before) {
    content: '—';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: #3D5E35;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  :global(.proj-quotes) {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }
  :global(.proj-quotes li) {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #3A3A3A;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    background: #EAEAEA;
    border-left: 3px solid #3D5E35;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* ── Architecture flow diagram ── */
  .proj-flow {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    padding: 20px;
    background: #EAEAEA;
    border: 1px solid #C4C4C4;
    border-radius: 6px;
  }
  .proj-flow-step {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #1A1A1A;
    background: #F5F5F0;
    border: 1px solid #C4C4C4;
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    text-align: center;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }
  .proj-flow-step small {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: #6B6B6B;
  }
  .proj-flow-arrow {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: #3D5E35;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  /* ── Technology stack grid ── */
  .proj-stack-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 28px;
  }
  .proj-stack-label {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #3D5E35;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .proj-stack-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 6px;
  }
  .proj-stack-list li {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: #2A2A2A;
  }

  /* ── Data disclaimer note ── */
  .proj-note {
    background: #F2EBDA;
    border: 1px solid #D8C89A;
    border-left: 4px solid #8B6914;
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 18px 22px;
    max-width: 68ch;
  }
  .proj-note-title {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #8B6914;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .proj-note p {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #3A3A3A;
  }

  .proj-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: 12px;
  }
  .proj-external {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #3D5E35;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .proj-external:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .proj-external-primary {
    padding: 10px 20px;
    color: #F0EAE0;
    background: #3D5E35;
    border-radius: 4px;
    transition: background 0.15s;
  }
  .proj-external-primary:hover { background: #2E4828; text-decoration: none; }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .proj-stack-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .proj-stack-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .proj-flow { flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; }
    .proj-flow-arrow { transform: rotate(90deg); text-align: center; }
  }
</style>
